BT /F1 9 Tf 12 TL 48 783 Td
(is a complete intersection of degrees2 and3 in P^3. Its quadric is) Tj T*
( Q=x2^2-x1*x3+x1*x4+x3*x4.) Tj T*
(Its symmetric Gram matrix has rank4 and determinant -1/4. Adjunction) Tj T*
(identifies O\(1\) with the canonical sheaf, so C is nonhyperelliptic.) Tj T*
(A rational degree3 pencil would give a rational ruling of this quadric:) Tj T*
(the canonical spans of the general fibres are lines contained in Q.) Tj T*
(But the nonsquare discriminant means the two rulings are exchanged) Tj T*
(over Q\(i\), and neither is defined over Q. This excludes a Q-map of) Tj T*
(degree3. Genus4 excludes degree1. This also agrees with the published) Tj T*
(classification: Hasegawa--Shimura, Trigonal modular curves, and) Tj T*
(Jeon, Modular curves with infinitely many cubic points, Theorem2.3.) Tj T*
(The latter is available at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnt.2020.09.006 .) Tj T*
(Here rational gonality is essential; C is trigonal over Q\(i\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Suppose Z had a Q-map u:Z -> P^1 of degree d=2 or3. Since g\(Z\)=8) Tj T*
(exceeds \(d-1\)^2, its degree-d pencil is unique, by the) Tj T*
(Castelnuovo--Severi inequality. For these prime degrees, two distinct) Tj T*
(pencils give a birational map to a bidegree\(d,d\) curve in P^1 x P^1,) Tj T*
(which would have genus at most\(d-1\)^2.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Thus u s=alpha u for alpha in PGL_2\(Q\), with alpha^2=1. If alpha=1,) Tj T*
(u factors through C. This is impossible for d=3 by parity, and for) Tj T*
(d=2 because it would give a degree1 map C -> P^1. If alpha is a) Tj T*
(nontrivial involution, pass to quotients to obtain a Q-map) Tj T*
(C -> P^1/<alpha> of degree d. The quotient of P^1 is rational over Q) Tj T*
(\(for example by Luroth's theorem\), so this contradicts the preceding) Tj T*
(paragraph. Degree1 is excluded by genus. Therefore gon_Q\(Z\)>=4.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. THE CONTRADICTION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The principal divisor sD_Z-D_Z has positive part of degree at most3.) Tj T*
(The gonality bound from Section4 forces it to be zero. Thus sD_Z=D_Z.) Tj T*
(The three distinct geometric points of D_Z form one Galois orbit, and) Tj T*
(s commutes with Galois. All its orbits on those three points have the) Tj T*
(same length. Since the length divides2 and must divide3, it is1.) Tj T*
(All three points are fixed by s.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Riemann--Hurwitz for the double cover Z -> C gives exactly) Tj T*
(       \(2*g\(Z\)-2\)-2*\(2*g\(C\)-2\)=14-12=2) Tj T*
(geometric fixed points of s in characteristic zero. Three fixed points) Tj T*
(are impossible. This excludes the last reduction at61 and proves) Tj T*
(61 is not in S\(15\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(6. REPLAY AND SCOPE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
